# Structure from Motion: photographs in, a 3D reconstruction out.
#
# Drop a folder of images on the palette as a GROUP -- hold ALT while dropping
# several -- then right-click the group and "Sort by filename" so the order is
# the capture order. The matchers chain against neighbours, so order matters.
#
# Or drop a VIDEO on the "group" row: it replaces the photographs with frames
# kept each time the view has moved far enough -- more where the camera moves
# fast, fewer where it is slow, the sharpest near each point. Walk slowly around
# the subject with exposure locked; see README for capture tips. A phone's
# field of view differs from fountain-P11's -- about 33 degrees against 58.
#
# NOTHING NEEDS SETTING FOR EITHER. The field of view is estimated and then
# measured (see solve_cameras below), and frames that see the same thing from
# far apart are matched as well as neighbours (revisit, below), which is what
# closes the loop on a walk around a subject.
#
# WHAT EACH STAGE DOES, because the chain is long and every stage solves a
# genuinely different problem:
#
#   detect_dad       finds features: a network trained to pick points that are
#                    found again in other views, because repeatability is the
#                    whole game -- a point that is not found again in the next
#                    frame contributes nothing. detect_akaze is the designed
#                    alternative; see the measurements at `feat` below.
#
#   describe_dedode  describes them: a network's 128-float description of
#                    each point, far more distinctive than a designed one.
#
#   match_ann        pairs them, exactly on the GPU for float descriptors. `chain` matches each frame against its
#                    neighbours rather than against frame 0, and `window` says
#                    how many neighbours. More than one is what gives the view
#                    graph loops, and loops are what let rotation averaging
#                    remove drift instead of merely accumulating it.
#
#   relative_pose    the ESSENTIAL matrix for each pair, and the relative
#                    rotation inside it. This is the stage that turns pixel
#                    correspondences into 3D geometry.
#
#                    `fov` is the most consequential setting in the script.
#                    The essential matrix is only an essential matrix in
#                    CALIBRATED coordinates, so a wrong field of view biases
#                    every recovered rotation. Read it off the lens where you
#                    can; sweep it and watch the rotation residual where you
#                    cannot.
#
#   match_guided     DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS CHAIN. A second matching pass
#                    guided by the solved geometry finds 18% more matches, and
#                    on repetitive texture a small fraction of them are the
#                    same wall matched one brick course over. Those are
#                    mutually consistent, so they survive RANSAC, chain into
#                    tracks and triangulate into a PHANTOM SECOND WALL --
#                    measured on fountain-P11, where the cloud goes from one
#                    sheet to two. 287 wrong matches out of 15544 is enough.
#                    See the header of match_guided.cpp; it needs a third-view
#                    check before it can be trusted here.
#
#   build_tracks     chains pairwise matches into TRACKS -- one 3D point and
#                    every image that saw it -- by union-find. Global SfM needs
#                    the whole correspondence graph before it can solve
#                    anything, which is why this is a stage rather than a
#                    by-product the way it is in incremental pipelines.
#
#   rotation_average one global orientation per camera, consistent with every
#                    relative rotation at once. L1-IRLS reweights an edge by
#                    how far it disagrees with the rest of the graph, so a
#                    mismatched pair that survived RANSAC stops pulling every
#                    camera connected through it.
#
#   global_position  where the cameras ARE. Two views fix a translation only in
#                    DIRECTION, never in length, so this has to recover the
#                    consistent set of lengths from the graph as a whole. The
#                    joint method solves for camera and point positions
#                    together, which stays determined when the cameras are
#                    collinear -- a dolly or a straight transect -- where
#                    classic translation averaging is ill-posed.
#
#   triangulate      3D points from the solved cameras, with three rejections:
#                    too little parallax (the depth would be arbitrary), behind
#                    a camera, or reprojecting too far from where it was seen.
#
#   bundle_adjust_sfm  moves EVERYTHING at once -- cameras, points, focal --
#                    to minimise reprojection error in pixels. That is the only
#                    measure that says a reconstruction is right; every stage
#                    before it minimises a proxy.
#
#   plane_sweep      DENSE depth, one map per frame, by testing a stack of
#                    candidate depths at every pixel and keeping the one the
#                    neighbouring views agree with.
#
#   fuse_depth       those maps into one cloud, keeping a depth only where
#                    several cameras independently measured the same surface.
#                    This is where the sparse few thousand points become
#                    several hundred thousand.
#
# READING THE STATUS LINE, in the order things go wrong:
#
#   * matches per pair below ~100: the view graph is too thin to average.
#   * mean track length near 2: nothing is chaining, so every point is a bare
#     pair and the geometry has no redundancy.
#   * rotation residual above a degree or two: some edges are wrong. It climbs
#     with the matching window, because wide baselines share less and the
#     eight-point algorithm is weaker there.
#   * reprojection rms after bundle: under a pixel is good.

frames = image("group")

# NO LONGER REQUIRED, and kept for speed rather than correctness.
#
# This used to be load-bearing. A detector's thresholds are calibrated around
# a megapixel, so at 3072 px AKAZE found far more than max_features, threw
# nearly all of them away by RESPONSE RANK -- which clusters on high-contrast
# relief and starves the flat regions carrying most of the frame -- and the
# reconstruction collapsed to a few hundred points. The comment here used to
# say DO NOT TURN THIS OFF, in capitals, and it was right at the time.
#
# detect_akaze grew a `max_dim` since, which downscales what the DETECTOR
# sees while leaving the image alone. That fixed the cause, and the warning
# with it. Measured on fountain-P11:
#
#   resize   sparse points   dense points   sweep, GPU   sweep, CPU
#     0.4             2032         476619          2 s         15 s
#     off             2894        2507256         18 s         33 s
#
# (Dense counts from bench_sfm at the script's settings -- the "off" row from
# before plane_sweep's min_bracket, which costs about a tenth; the app has produced
# around six million at full resolution with looser fuse_depth settings.)
#
# FULL RESOLUTION IS NOW BETTER, not worse: 40% more sparse points and five
# times the dense cloud, because the sweep correlates real pixels rather than
# a quarter of them.
#
# So this is a speed control. The dense stages cost roughly the pixel count,
# and millions of points is more than most uses need -- 0.4 is a reasonable
# default for working quickly and turning it off is the right move for a
# final reconstruction. Both are correct; they differ in how long you wait.
small = params( resize, scale = 0.4 )( frames )

# LEARNED FEATURES BY DEFAULT: DaD finds the points and DeDoDe describes them
# -- the detector and descriptor of LoMa (2026), run on tglab's own kernels.
# Measured against the AKAZE line below, everything else as in this script:
#
#                          inliers/pair   points   ray residual   focal
#   fountain-P11  AKAZE         437         1998      0.171 deg   57.9 deg
#                 learned      3998        10131      0.035       58.1
#   castle-P19    AKAZE         299         2175      0.982       57.9
#                 learned      2044        14450      0.130       58.2
#
# (Both captures are calibrated at 58.2 degrees.) On a hand-held video of a
# chrome tape measure the weakest link in the sequence went from 39
# triangulated tracks to 389 -- the difference between one reconstruction
# and two copies of the subject.
#
# The two networks take about a fifth of a second a frame on an RTX 4070 Ti
# (and several seconds on the CPU, without a device). Their weights live in
# models/ beside tglab; see third_party/models/README.md.
#
# DaD keeps the 10000 strongest points after non-maximum suppression. It
# spreads them evenly by construction -- it was trained to pick points that
# are found again -- so the cap does not starve flat regions the way a
# designed detector's response ranking does (see the AKAZE notes below).
feat  = params( describe_dedode )( params( detect_dad, max_features = 10000 )( small ) )

# THE DESIGNED ALTERNATIVE: swap this in for the line above to compare.
#
# MORE FEATURES THAN THE DETECTOR'S DEFAULT, because 5000 is a cap tuned for
# a single image and SfM is not that. A detector that hits its cap discards
# candidates by response rank rather than by which points the NEXT frame will
# also find. Measured on castle-P19: cap 5000 gave 359 inliers a pair and 987
# points, 10000 gave 505 and 1669, 20000 no more -- at this resolution AKAZE
# finds fewer than 10000.
#
# colour = 1 APPENDS 84 BITS OF OPPONENT-COLOUR to each descriptor, which AKAZE
# otherwise throws away: it describes a greyscale patch, so two bricks of
# different colour with the same relief are indistinguishable to it. On
# fountain-P11 it took inliers a pair from 582 to 685 and halved the worst
# rotation residual. Turn it off for a monochrome capture.
#
# feat = params( detect_akaze, max_features = 10000, colour = 1 )( small )

# CHAIN IS NOT THE DEFAULT, and leaving it off is the single easiest way to
# get nothing out of this script.
#
# Without it every frame matches against frame 0, which is right for a bracket
# of one scene and useless for a walk-around: frame 18 and frame 0 of a circuit
# share no view at all. Measured on castle-P19 with chain off, 17 of 18 pairs
# were rejected for want of correspondences and triangulation had nothing left
# to work with.
#
# window = 2 MATCHES EACH FRAME TO THE TWO BEFORE IT, which is what gives a
# track more than two views -- and the number of views per track is what
# decides whether the depths are any good.
#
# A two-view point is a bare ray intersection with nothing to contradict it,
# and at triangulate's 2-degree parallax threshold its DEPTH uncertainty is 29
# times its lateral uncertainty. Half a pixel of match error then becomes a
# large depth error, and a flat wall reconstructs as several parallel sheets at
# different depths -- which is exactly how this presented on fountain-P11,
# looking down at a brick wall that had become three walls.
#
# Measured there: window 2 removes most of the phantom planes.
#
# THIS IS NOT UNIVERSALLY BETTER. On castle-P19 the rotation residual climbs
# with the window (0, 4 and 15 degrees at 1, 2 and 3) because that capture
# turns ~22 degrees per frame, so frames two apart share very little and the
# extra edges are mostly bad. fountain-P11 turns ~12 degrees per frame and the
# skip edges are good. Match the window to how fast the camera turns: check
# `mean rotation` in the relative_pose report, and if frames two apart are more
# than about 30 degrees apart, stay at 1.
#
# revisit = 3 ALSO MATCHES EACH FRAME TO THE THREE FRAMES ELSEWHERE THAT LOOK
# MOST LIKE IT: the end of a walk around a subject meeting its start, a sweep
# passing the same view twice. Without it a loop never closes and its error
# piles up -- a 100-frame video circling a toy cat came out as a spiral whose
# end sat three radii from its start. Each revisit is then CHECKED by
# relative_pose against the rotation the neighbour chain predicts, because on
# repetitive structure frames look alike without being the same view: castle-
# P19's window bays produced revisits that took its ray residual from 0.8
# degrees to 4.3 before the check, and 0.97 after it (23 of 35 dropped).
#
# A revisit is a RETURN: a frame outside the stretch of the walk that still
# looks like this one, with a stretch that does not between them. Revisits
# used to be simply the best-scoring frames outside the window, and in a
# video those are frames 3 to 10 away -- so on a 1576-frame room scan none of
# them closed a loop. Measured with it on, nothing else set:
#
#   capture        revisits kept / dropped   points    reprojection
#   fountain-P11        0 / 0  (11 photos)    10789       0.45 px
#   castle-P19          4 / 4                 15106       0.44
#   cat video          68 / 0, ~150 apart     49245       0.76     one closed ring
#   face video         10 / 10                20303       0.90
#
# Against the old choice: 39% more points on the cat and 52% on the face, the
# worst rotation edge 1.8 degrees where it was 5.7 on the cat, and 9.4 where
# it was 14.5 on castle-P19.
pairs = params( match_ann, chain = 1, window = 2, revisit = 3 )( feat )

# LoMa's LEARNED MATCHER instead of nearest neighbour: a transformer that
# rewrites every point's description by attention -- to its own image, then
# to the other -- before matching, so repeated structure is resolved by
# context. It needs the descriptors raw, and few enough points that its
# quadratic attention stays affordable:
#
# feat  = params( describe_dedode, normalise = 0 )( params( detect_dad, max_features = 4096 )( small ) )
# pairs = params( match_ann, method = 1, chain = 1, window = 2, revisit = 3 )( feat )
#
# MEASURED against nearest neighbour on the same 4096 points a frame:
#
#                       points       reprojection   worst rotation   matching
#   castle-P19   NN       7967          0.38 px           --            1.2 s
#                LoMa     8765          0.44             --           10.9
#   IMG_1535     NN      26297          0.83            10.8 deg       17 s
#                LoMa    25385          0.88             4.8           91 s
#
# Better pairwise geometry -- the rotation graph's worst edge halves -- and
# about as many points, for five to ten times the matching time. `layers = 3`
# keeps 95% of the points at half the cost. Not the default, then: the
# nearest-neighbour line above, with 10000 points, still reconstructs more.

# NO align_features here, unlike the panorama script. It fits a homography,
# which describes a pure camera ROTATION -- exactly what SfM does not have.
# relative_pose runs its own RANSAC against the essential matrix instead, and
# writes those inliers back for build_tracks.
# --- THE CAMERAS -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# solve_cameras runs the whole chain that turns matched frames into cameras
# and a sparse cloud:
#
#   relative_pose     the essential matrix for each pair, and the rotation
#                     and direction inside it
#   build_tracks      pairwise matches joined into multi-frame tracks
#   rotation_average  one orientation per camera from all the pairs
#   global_position   where the cameras are, jointly with the points
#   triangulate, bundle_adjust_sfm, twice
#
# Each of those is still a stage of its own, and can be chained by hand to
# compare one against an alternative. This one exists so a capture simply
# reconstructs, with nothing to set.
#
# THE FOCAL LENGTH IS FOUND, NOT ASKED FOR. It is the most consequential
# number in the chain -- on a video walked around a toy cat, solving at 42
# degrees where the lens was about 33 made the rotations fail to close the
# circle by 31 degrees and the reconstruction broke apart. relative_pose
# estimates a starting value from the matches, bundle adjustment measures the
# real one, and solve_cameras solves again with it until the two agree.
# Measured with nothing given:
#
#   capture        estimate   settled at   truth
#   fountain-P11      51.6       57.8       58.2 (calibrated)
#   castle-P19        60.8       58.4       58.2 (calibrated)
#   cat video         30.3       33.3       ~33 (the loop closes there)
#
# If the lens is known, fov_deg on solve_cameras skips the estimate.
#
# ITS OTHER SETTINGS, each measured:
#
#   min_length 3      no two-view tracks. A track seen twice reprojects
#                     perfectly -- it was placed to -- whatever its depth.
#                     On fountain-P11 the cloud's extent fell from 4.95 to
#                     1.42 units, and the phantom planes went with it.
#   max_distance 3    points no further than 3 camera-ring radii: right for a
#                     walk around a subject, where anything beyond is a stray.
#                     Raise it for an open scene shot from one side.
#   triangulate and bundle twice: the first triangulation judges tracks
#                     against cameras nothing has refined yet; the second
#                     re-judges them. castle-P19: 1669 points at 2.5 px after
#                     one round, 2168 at 0.9 px after two.
cloud = params( solve_cameras )( pairs )

# --- DENSE RECONSTRUCTION ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything above produces a SPARSE cloud: a few thousand points, one per
# feature the detector happened to find and the matcher managed to chain. That
# is enough to recover where the cameras were, which is what it is for, and it
# is nowhere near enough to describe a surface.
#
# These two stages ask the question at every pixel instead.
#
#   plane_sweep   guesses a depth for each pixel and keeps the one whose
#                 neighbouring views agree with it. Outputs a depth map and a
#                 confidence map per frame, both viewable -- the confidence
#                 map is where a bad sweep confesses.
#
#   fuse_depth    turns N depth maps into ONE cloud. The maps disagree, and
#                 resolving that is the whole job: a depth is kept only when
#                 other cameras independently measured the same surface in the
#                 same place, and agreeing measurements merge into one point
#                 rather than N near-duplicates.
#
# Measured on fountain-P11: a few thousand sparse points become several
# hundred thousand dense ones, a ~200x increase.
#
# min_views = 3 RATHER THAN THE DEFAULT 2, because a third independent
# measurement is what separates a real surface from a coincidence. Two views
# agreeing happens often enough on repetitive texture to leave visible noise
# in the cloud; requiring three removes most of it, at a real cost in
# coverage. Raise it further on a capture with many overlapping views.
#
# THE SWEEP IS NO LONGER THE EXPENSIVE STAGE. It correlates a window per
# pixel per plane per neighbour, which cost 110 s on the CPU originally, 15 s
# after the warp-once-per-plane rewrite, and 2 s on the GPU -- against ~13 s
# for the detector, matching and SfM chain above it. Without a usable device
# it falls back to the CPU and the report line says so. Lower `planes` if it
# matters; the cost is linear in it.
#
# Delete these two lines and the "dense" viewer to get the sparse pipeline
# back -- nothing above depends on them.
dense = params( plane_sweep, planes = 48 )( cloud, small )
fused = params( fuse_depth, min_views = 3 )( cloud, dense )

# --- GAUSSIAN SPLATS -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT: training is minutes where everything above is seconds, and
# while tuning the solve you want to see the cameras, not wait out a fit.
# Tick `enabled` under init_splats to switch the whole branch on -- training,
# carving and rendering all read from it, so they go off and on with it and
# their own boxes can stay ticked. (Unticking plane_sweep does the same for
# the dense branch and everything after it.)
#
# One Gaussian per dense point: a small disc lying in the surface its
# neighbours fit, as wide as the gap to them -- the starting point training
# optimises from.
splats = params( init_splats, enabled = 0 )( fused )

# TRAINING: the splats fitted to the photographs. Each iteration renders one
# camera, compares it with that camera's photograph, and nudges every
# Gaussian's position, shape, rotation, opacity and colour toward a better
# match. The report gives L1 and PSNR over all the cameras before and after.
#
# `iterations` IS THE KNOB. On the GPU an iteration is about 50 ms for half a
# million Gaussians at `downscale` 2; without a device it falls back to the
# CPU at about 0.8 s, and the report says which ran. The paper runs 30000; a
# few hundred here already fits well. 0 trains nothing and just measures the
# untrained fit.
#
# DENSIFICATION IS ON: every 50 iterations Gaussians that cannot follow the
# detail under them are split or cloned, and near-transparent ones removed.
# Measured on fountain-P11:
#
#   iterations   PSNR after   GPU     CPU
#          100     13.7 dB     7 s    85 s
#          300     17.6 dB    15 s   308 s
#         3000     31.2 dB   143 s
#
# Those are scores on the photographs being trained on, which rewards fitting
# them exactly. Setting `holdout = 5` leaves cameras 2 and 7 out and scores
# them separately -- how it looks from somewhere it was not fitted to:
#
#   iterations   depth   training   held out
#          300      no    18.0 dB    17.1 dB
#         1000      no    25.0       22.8
#         1000     yes    24.4       24.0
#         3000      no    31.2       23.3     <- mostly memorising
#         3000     yes    30.7       24.6
#
# Past 1000 the training score climbs six more decibels while the held-out one
# gains half of one: the extra fit is almost all to the eleven photographs
# themselves, and shows between them as false colour and smears. 1000 is the
# default for that trade -- a third of the time for most of what is real.
#
# DEPTH SUPERVISION is the third input: the sweep's depth maps. Rendered depth
# is pulled toward them (depth_weight, 0.3), which pins Gaussians to the
# surfaces the cameras measured instead of wherever paints the right colour.
# Leave `dense` out to train on colour alone. Where the sweep measured nothing
# -- the blank wall on the right -- colour decides as before.
#
# `passes` SCALES IT WITH THE CLIP: each iteration looks at one view, so a
# fixed count trains a 1576-frame walk around a room at two thirds of a look
# per view -- which made its splats worse than untrained. With passes = 16 the
# count is 16 x the views (x1.5 with reflections) and `iterations` is the
# floor: 1000 for fountain and a 60-frame face, about 2900 for a 179-frame
# orbit, 25000 for the room.
trained = params( train_splats, iterations = 1000, passes = 16 )( splats, small, dense )

# FLOATERS, removed by free-space carving against the sweep's depth maps. A
# Gaussian is dropped when more than `max_seen_through` cameras measured a
# surface clearly BEHIND it -- they looked straight through where it claims
# to be. These are Gaussians training used to paint the pale, textureless
# wall on the right, which only a few left-hand views see: correct from
# those views, floating in front of the fountain from everywhere else.
# 1 removes most of them; 0 is stricter but punches holes in the basin,
# because then one camera's depth noise is enough to condemn a Gaussian.
# With many cameras a count alone is too easy to reach, so the dissenters
# must also be a fifth of the cameras that measured there (max_seen_fraction):
# on the 100-frame videos that took carving from removing 62-77% of the
# Gaussians, holes and all, to 6-11%.
# It also removes LARGE Gaussians that fewer than `large_min_views` cameras
# have in view: at the edges of the capture nothing stops one smearing.
carved = params( carve_splats )( trained, dense )

# The trained splats rendered from each camera, through the same rasteriser
# training scored against -- for comparing frame N with render N directly.
# The "frames" and "render" viewers step through their frames together.
#
# OFF BY DEFAULT: it is for checking a fit, not part of making one, and on
# a hundred-frame video it is the better part of a minute. Tick `enabled`
# under render_splats to validate.
rendered = params( render_splats, enabled = 0 )( carved )

display( small, "frames" )
display( cloud, "reconstruction" )
display( fused, "dense" )
display( carved, "splats" )
display( rendered, "render" )

# EXPORT, for viewing elsewhere: the carved splats in the standard 3DGS .ply
# layout that SuperSplat and other splat viewers read (see view_ply.tgl to
# bring one back in). Written when the app is asked to save, not on every
# run. `fused` or `cloud` save as plain coloured points.
#
#   save( carved, "fountain_splats.ply" )
